    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Math has always been my safe space. While art makes me feel free, math offers structure and clarity. My dad’s PhD in sciences made him push me to excel, but it also created pressure. Now, I see it as a tool—logical, precise, and rewarding when things click. It’s not just numbers; it’s a language I enjoy speaking.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, absolutely. My high neuroticism score means worry creeps in when things get complex. I fear failing or not understanding, especially since my parents have such different views on education. Sometimes, late nights before exams make me doubt myself, but I push through because I need to succeed.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I tried it a few times to clarify difficult concepts. It helped break down problems, but sometimes it felt too robotic for me. I prefer talking to a friend or teacher who can explain with empathy. AI is useful, but I need human connection to really grasp the material emotionally.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, identify the equation, like ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, calculate the discriminant: b² - 4ac. If it’s positive, you get two real roots. Apply the quadratic formula: (-b ± √discriminant) / 2a. Finally, check your answers by plugging them back in. It’s just logic in action.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Start by finding the derivative of f(x), which gives you f'(x). Set f'(x) equal to zero to locate where the slope is flat. Solve for x, which gives you the x-coordinates of the points. Then, plug those x-values back into f(x) to get the corresponding y-coordinates. Those pairs are your stationary points.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data dimensions by finding main patterns. First, standardize your data. Then, compute the covariance matrix. Find eigenvalues and eigenvectors to see which directions hold most variance. Keep components with high variance. Don’t get anxious—it’s just a tool. Take it one step at a time.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can create personalized practice problems and explain concepts in simple terms. They can adapt to each student’s pace, offering support when needed. But they should complement, not replace, human teachers. Emotional connection is vital, especially for students like me who need reassurance while learning."
